#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124969 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4x+0-0)\\,i+(0+4y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =4x\\,i+(4y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =4(0)\\,i+(4(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∇f(0,1)}{‖∇f(0,1)‖}=\\frac{3\\,j}{3}=j\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124970 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2xy + 3y^{2} = x^{2} + 4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Write the curve as F(x,y)=0. A tangent is (F_y,-F_x); its dot product with the given vector is $2 x - 2 y$. Solving this with the curve equation gives $(-1,-1), (1,1)$, and the tangent is nonzero at each point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124971 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $6xy=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(10x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(10-6(1))\\,i+(2(1)-6)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,1)=⟨4,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{4} & =\\frac{y-1}{-4} \\\\ x-1 & =-y+1 \\\\ x+y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124972 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(0, 1)$ for the function $f(x, y) = 1 - 4x^{2} - y^{2}$." :question/worked-solution "At $(0,1)$ the function value is $0$. Setting $1-4x^2-y^2=0$ gives the level curve $4x^2+y^2=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124973 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ at the point $(2,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}\\text{ }i-2y\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,0,1) & =\\frac{(2)}{2}\\,i-2(0)\\text{ }j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =i-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(2,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{∇}f(2,0,1)=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124976 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3x+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)} & =3(1)+6(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =3(x-1) \\\\ z & =3x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124977 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6(x-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,-3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-3) & =6(2-1)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,-3) & ={f}_{x}(2,-3)(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6x-12 \\\\ z & =6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-3 \\\\ z &=6x-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6x-9$ (or $6x-z-9=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-6⟩=⟨1,0,6⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124978 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2x+4y+5xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(5y+2)\\text{ }i+(5x+4)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(5\\cdot 1+2)\\text{ }i+(5\\cdot 2+4)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =7i+14j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,1)}{‖∇f(2,1)‖} & =\\frac{7i+14j}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(14)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7i+14j}{7\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124979 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}-y$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(6x+2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4xy-1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,1) & =(6(-1)+2(1)^{2})\\,i+(4(-1)(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-5\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-4,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-4,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124980 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}y=xy+4.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =xy+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-xy & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,2)={f}_{y}(2,2)\\,i-{f}_{x}(2,2)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-xy)\\,j \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-x)\\,i-(2xy-y)\\,j \\\\ t(2,2) & =({2}^{2}-2)\\,i-(2(2)(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i-6\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124985 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =\\frac{1}{2-{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(1,2)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =y-{x}^{2} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(1,2)$ is the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124987 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z = f(x, y)$. The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y = 0$. Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\frac{π}{2}$ lies on $C$, and that $f_{x} = y\\cos x + \\cos y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=0$ fixes $y$, so the slope of the intersection curve is $f_x$. At $P$, $$f_x(\\pi/2,0)=0\\cos(\\pi/2)+\\cos0=1.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125014 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}x$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}x & =c \\\\ xy({x}^{2}-y) & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=\\pm 1$ then the level curves are $xy({x}^{2}-y)=\\pm 1,$ which are not lines.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curves are $xy({x}^{2}-y)=0,$ which consists of the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$, and the parabola $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nSo, only statement “$c=0$ gives a parabola and the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125020 :question/problem "Given $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xyz}$, find $∇f(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }\\mathbf{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125022 :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that  $g(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x+2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x+2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+2}}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{yz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\cdot z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2y} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x+2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{yz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\cdot y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+2}}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{2z}\\text{ }\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125023 :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =y+{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =x+2xy+0 \\\\  & =x+2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z)=(y+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+2xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇h(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(x+2xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+{y}^{2} &=0 \\\\ x+2xy &=0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe first equation can be written as $y(1+y)=0$, which gives $y=0$ or $y=-1.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2x(0) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2x(-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125024 :question/problem "Find $f(x, y, z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)$, where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$." :question/worked-solution "Integrate $f_x=2xy$ with respect to $x$: $f=x^2y+g(y,z)$. Matching $f_y=x^2+2z$ gives $g_y=2z$, so $g=2yz+h(z)$. Finally $f_z=2y+h'(z)=2y$, hence $h$ is constant. Therefore $$f=x^2y+2yz+C.$$ Its three partial derivatives reproduce all components of $F$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125028 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(e,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(e,\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}{e},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{e},\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\cdot (-1)}{e},\\,1\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction of $∇g(e,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(e,\\pi )‖ & =‖⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{e}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125029 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x, y) = (x + y^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-2)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2, 2)$." :question/worked-solution "The direction from $A$ to $B$ is $\\langle3,4\\rangle$, with unit vector $u=\\langle3,4\\rangle/5$. Since $\\nabla f=\\langle2(x+y^2),4y(x+y^2)\\rangle$, we have $\\nabla f(-1,-2)=\\langle6,-24\\rangle$. Therefore $$D_uf=\\frac{18-96}{5}=-\\frac{78}{5}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125038 :question/problem "Given that $w(r,\\theta )=r(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ),$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{∂w}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125039 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125040 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =\\left(\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\left(\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125061 :question/problem "Find $f(x, y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$, where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}y\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}$." :question/worked-solution "Integrate $f_x=\\cos x$: $f=\\sin x+g(y)$. Then $f_y=g'(y)=\\sin y$ gives $g(y)=-\\cos y+C$. Thus $$f(x,y)=\\sin x-\\cos y+C,$$ whose gradient is exactly $\\langle\\cos x,\\sin y\\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125073 :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x, y)g(x, y)$ if $f(x, y) = \\ln x\\ln y$ and $g(x, y) = x^{2}y^{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "The product is $fg=x^2y^2\\ln x\\ln y$, defined for $x>0$ and $y>0$. Applying the product rule separately to each variable gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(fg)_x&=xy^2\\ln y(2\\ln x+1),\\\\\n(fg)_y&=x^2y\\ln x(2\\ln y+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\nabla(fg)=xy^2\\ln y(2\\ln x+1)i+x^2y\\ln x(2\\ln y+1)j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125074 :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)k \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2y)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,-1,1) & =(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\text{ }i+[(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+2(-1)]\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-i-2\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x}{-1}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\ x=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125075 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})-3{y}^{3}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\sqrt{\\pi },2).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\sqrt{\\pi },2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})-3{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})-3{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i-9{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(\\sqrt{\\pi },2) & =-2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡((\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2})i-9(2)^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot 0\\,i-36\\,j \\\\  & =-36\\,j \\\\  & =⟨0,-36⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(\\sqrt{\\pi },2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\sqrt{\\pi },\\,y-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,-36⟩\\cdot ⟨x-\\sqrt{\\pi },\\,y-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125078 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+5x+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(y+5)\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(-1+5)\\,i+(1)\\,j+2(2)\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+j+4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125179 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2{x}^{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2{x}^{2}) & =c \\\\ y-2{x}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ y & =2{x}^{2}+{e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of parabolas with the vertex at $(0,{e}^{c}).$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/65/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125183 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,-1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}(2xy-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,-1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1) & =(1)^{2}(2(1)(-1)-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,-1) & ={f}_{y}(1,-1)(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3y-3 \\\\ z & =-3y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,-1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-3y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-3y-1$ (or $-3y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-3,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j-3\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125189 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-2yz+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\text{ }i+(2xy-2z)\\text{ }j+(-2y+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+(2(1)(-1)-2(1))\\,j+(-2(-1)+1)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i-4j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125190 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(2(2)-(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨5,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{5} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-4} \\\\ -4(x-2) & =5(y+1) \\\\ -4x-5y+8-5 & =0 \\\\ -4x-5y+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+5y-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125191 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})={f}_{y}(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\ t(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))j \\\\  & =-i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125192 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $9x^{2}y = 3y^{3} - 6x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Write the curve as F(x,y)=0. A tangent is (F_y,-F_x); its dot product with the given vector is $9 x^{2} - 9 y^{2}$. Solving this with the curve equation gives $(-1,1), (0,0), (1,-1)$, and the tangent is nonzero at each point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125220 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}=\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{{2}^{3}-{2}^{2}\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{z({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{1}{z} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125221 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0}{\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy}-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{xy} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y} & =\\frac{(0-1)(\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}+1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125222 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{3}+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=-x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125223 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot {2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz(x-y)}{-(x-y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz}{-x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz}{-x-y} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{-2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125224 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{4-4}{\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\sqrt{4}+\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125229 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{cos}z+y\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-x\\operatorname{sin}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125243 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z)r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
